stratumak (0.1.3) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * A .comp can now declare the external libraries it needs: pkgconfig for a
    library that ships a .pc file, cflags and ldflags for one that does not.
    modcompile --compile links them, --deps prints them for a packager to
    turn into Build-Depends, and a missing module is reported against the
    line that asked for it rather than as an undefined symbol at load time.
    Before this, a module needing anything beyond libc and libm had to be
    built from a Makefile around --preprocess.
  * cflags and ldflags carry no shell: $(...), backticks and $VAR are
    refused rather than passed through, because modcompile --install runs as
    root and a .comp is data. The dynamic-linker tokens $ORIGIN, $LIB and
    $PLATFORM are allowed, since -Wl,-rpath,$ORIGIN is how a module finds a
    vendor library shipped beside it.
  * .comp options are a whitelist now, so an option that is parsed and never
    read cannot pass silently. extra_compile_args and extra_link_args -- both
    dropped on the floor until now -- name their replacement and stop the
    build, as does anything unrecognised. The classic options this tree
    ignores (fp, personality, default_personality) still build, with a
    warning; the ones it cannot honour (count_function, constructable,
    rtapi_app and their kin) are refused with a message saying so.
    docs/src/hal/comp.adoc's option list is rewritten to match what
    modcompile actually consults, and the out8 example is ported to it.
  * An int modparam is parsed with strtol rather than atoi, so ioaddr=0x378
    reaches the module as 0x378. It had stopped at the 'x' and handed the
    module 0 with nothing said about it, which is the shape of value an I/O
    address most often has.
  * An M100-M199 handler's return value reaches the interpreter again, so a
    G-code program can read it from #5399 -- that is how a program learns
    what a handler decided. The getter behind it was a stub returning 0, so
    the 0-is-success, 32-63-is-a-user-result convention the handler interface
    documents was written but not wired.
  * Modules that modcompile builds are compiled with -Werror by default, and
    --cflags and --print-make-inc advertise the same, so a project driving
    its own Makefile is held to what modcompile would apply to the same file.
    $STMAK_CWARNFLAGS is the opt-out. The tree's own build takes the policy
    from --enable-werror, which CI passes and debian/rules does not, so a
    distro rebuild on a newer compiler cannot be broken by a new diagnostic.

 -- Sascha Ittner <sascha.ittner@modusoft.de>  Tue, 11 Aug 2026 17:18:22 +0200

stratumak (0.1.2) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * stratumak-dev now ships every generated GMI service header on the include
    path. Only interp_ctx_api.h and interp_ext_api.h reached it before, so an
    out-of-tree component talking to any other service failed to compile on a
    header the package already contained -- under share/stratumak/stmak/
    generated/, which is on no include path. Nothing else about the packaging
    changes; the headers follow from being in the installed header list.
  * The branding images moved out of the source-tree root to
    share/stratumak/images/. Installed paths and file names are unchanged:
    they still land flat in share/stratumak, so existing configurations,
    including those naming linuxcnc.gif as INTRO_GRAPHIC, are unaffected.
  * stepconf and pncconf resolve the wizard image and the application icon
    from that directory first, falling back to the installed layout as before.
    A run-in-place tree no longer stages a second copy of linuxcnc-wizard.gif.
  * Test coverage is now measured from the runtests integration suite driving
    a real stmakd, and reported for C and Go separately. Instrumentation is
    opt-in (COVERAGE=1) with no default, and no package build passes it, so a
    released binary is never instrumented.
  * README_LINUXCNC.md is removed. It was upstream's project readme, and the
    build instructions it was cited for are in docs/INSTALL.adoc.

 -- Sascha Ittner <sascha.ittner@modusoft.de>  Thu, 06 Aug 2026 17:27:54 +0200

stratumak (0.1.1) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * Release builds now take the plain version from the release tag. Tagging
    v0.1.0 and building it produced 0.1.0+git<count>.g<sha>: the snapshot
    suffix, which dpkg sorts *above* 0.1.0, so the release ranked below every
    snapshot leading up to it. debian/update-dch-from-git now also checks the
    tag against debian/changelog and VERSION and stops the build when they
    disagree, rather than shipping a package whose version names a different
    release than the tag it was built from.
  * modcompile add-gomod no longer copies an external Go module's development
    stubs into the stratuMAK source tree. It reads the directories from the
    module's own local replace directives and excludes them from the copy, and
    a reinstall removes stubs an earlier version left behind. They had been
    copied in and kept out of the build only because such a stub carries its
    own go.mod -- deleting that file would have compiled the stubs into stmakd
    beside the pkg/hal and pkg/inifile they shadow.
  * modcompile no longer tests external requirements against the pre-rename
    github.com/sittner/linuxcnc/ module path, which nothing has matched since
    the rename. A module that dropped its local replace would have written a
    self-require into stratuMAK's go.mod.

 -- Sascha Ittner <sascha.ittner@modusoft.de>  Thu, 06 Aug 2026 12:06:46 +0200

stratumak (0.1.0) UNRELEASED; urgency=medium

  * First release under the project's own name. stratumak is derived from
    LinuxCNC 2.9.8; the LinuxCNC packaging history it grew out of is kept for
    reference in debian/changelog.linuxcnc.
  * Renamed from linuxcnc-uspace to stratumak. The package
    Conflicts/Replaces/Provides the linuxcnc and linuxcnc-uspace names it takes
    over; internal paths are unchanged for now.
  * Version restarts at 0.1.0. The epoch is dropped with the old name: a
    renamed package has no upgrade ordering to preserve.
  * Debian packaging repaired -- the build had not completed since stratuMAK
    landed, and the package it would have produced carried no controller
    binary. See docs/dev/PRODUCTION_READINESS.md.

 -- Sascha Ittner <sascha.ittner@modusoft.de>  Fri, 31 Jul 2026 17:34:07 +0200
